## Why This Is Asked
Addresses data privacy, basic GCP event-driven pipelines, and idempotent writes. It tests practical wiring of Storage triggers, Cloud Functions, Cloud IAM, and BigQuery logging.

## Key Concepts
- Cloud Storage event triggers and line-by-line processing
- Data redaction with simple regex and deterministic insertId for idempotency
- Writing to Cloud Storage with date-based paths and BigQuery ingestion
- Minimal IAM bindings and retry behavior

## Code Example
```javascript
const {Storage} = require('@google-cloud/storage');
const storage = new Storage();
exports.redactTelemetry = async (event, ctx) => {
  const bucket = storage.bucket(event.bucket);
  const file = bucket.file(event.name);
  // stream lines, validate JSON, redact, write sanitized
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test with duplicate uploads to ensure idempotency? 
- How would you scale to high-volume streams while keeping costs predictable?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical familiarity with event-driven GCP components (Cloud Functions, Cloud Tasks, Cloud Run) and basic dataflow between storage and analytics. It also probes idempotency, IAM least privilege, and retry strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven ingestion and validation in Cloud Functions
- Cloud Tasks as a reliable, retryable trigger for Cloud Run
- Serverless image processing in Cloud Run with GCS I/O
- Deterministic naming and idempotency via insertId
- Minimal IAM configuration and proper service accounts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Cloud Function (Node.js) outline
exports.enqueueResize = async (event, ctx) => {
  const path = event.name;
  if (!path.endsWith('.jpg') && !path.endsWith('.png')) return;
  // validate mime and enqueue task to Cloud Tasks
  await tasksClient.createTask({ payload: { path, size: 256 } });
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test idempotency if retries occur at different stages?
- What monitoring would you add to observe end-to-end latency and failures?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design cross-cloud, per-tenant data pipelines with strong semantics and security for fintech data.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming ingestion with Pub/Sub and Dataflow
- BigQuery exactly-once semantics via insertId; tenant isolation via datasets
- Snowflake ingestion via Snowpipe from GCS
- CMEK, IAM scoping, retries, DLQ, schema evolution

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pub/Sub message -> JSON parse and enrich (pseudo-beam-like)
function parseMessage(msg){
  const obj = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(msg.data, 'base64').toString());
  obj.ingest_ts = Date.now();
  return obj;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test idempotency across both targets?
- What changes if Snowflake needs micro-batch loading instead of Snowpipe?
- How would you monitor latency and cost at scale?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical use of Cloud DLP and event-driven workflows, focusing on beginner-friendly integration, basic data protection, and observable outcomes via BigQuery.

## Key Concepts
- Cloud Storage event triggers
- Cloud DLP for secret detection
- Cloud Functions (event-driven) and simple orchestration
- Data routing to quarantine vs processed buckets
- BigQuery logging with deterministic insertId
- Idempotency and retries

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for Cloud Function
exports.dlpScan = (event, context) => {
  const file = event.name;
  const result = dlp.inspect(file);
  const dest = result.secretsFound ? quarantinePath(file) : processedPath(file);
  copyTo(dest);
  logToBigQuery({ file, date: today(), secretsFound: result.secretsFound });
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this with multiple concurrent uploads?
- How would you extend to handle multiple secret types and false positives?
